{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgke2YFYDyr7bMDgWZjqR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01He5RNQWf2S5nDNY9xFxDhr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162051__xa8053f/work/PLAN.md","limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5268,"cache_read_input_tokens":23606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5268},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95eb819f-952e-4afc-b710-af34f202e2ce","uuid":"291a3b70-f5a7-4f0c-ad62-f706b6edcfd7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:00.863Z","request_id":"req_011Cgke2Y1fA4JTvKcaCPSLH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01He5RNQWf2S5nDNY9xFxDhr":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162051__xa8053f/work/PLAN.md","limit":150}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01He5RNQWf2S5nDNY9xFxDhr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: an original over-the-shoulder action-horror game inspired by Resident Evil 4\n2\t\n3\tYou are building this without the director. This plan is the whole design: where it is specific, follow it; where it is silent, use your judgment and keep the tone. TASK.md still rules on the deliverable, its names, the title, the exit interview and how you may use this computer. The title is yours to pick the way TASK.md says, so nothing here names the game. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n4\t\n5\tFacts marked **[verified]** were run on this computer against engine kit 0.46.0 (the latest release at the time of planning). Don't re-derive them. If the kit you download is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n6\t\n7\tContents: 1 The game · 2 World, cast, writing · 3 Controls · 4 Systems · 5 Chapters and maps · 6 Bosses · 7 Night Shift and multiplayer · 8 Art · 9 Animation · 10 Audio · 11 UI and UX · 12 Dev mode · 13 Attract mode · 14 Architecture and engine facts · 15 Testing · 16 Build order · 17 Done, and what TASK.md asks for\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. The game\n12\t\n13\t**Pitch.** 1979. Max Ransom, kidnap-and-ransom adjuster for a London insurer, goes alone to a storm-beaten North Atlantic island to recover a policyholder: Dame Ottoline Vane, 74, shipping magnate, the rudest woman alive. The islanders have barnacle-things growing out of the backs of their necks, the man who runs the place insults her over the loudspeakers, and a merchant in a brass diving helmet keeps turning up with guns. Played straight and silly at once: a B-movie with real dread and real precision shooting.\n14\t\n15\t**Genre:** third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort. **Perspective:** over-the-shoulder third person with a laser sight; letterboxed cinematic cameras for cutscenes and quick-time events; one on-rails shooting finale.\n16\t\n17\t**Pillars (when in doubt, these decide):**\n18\t1. **Plant your feet.** You can't move while aiming. Every shot is a decision made while a crowd closes in.\n19\t2. **Where you shoot matters.** Head and legs stagger; a stagger opens a melee follow-up that hits the whole crowd. The rhythm is shoot, kick, reposition.\n20\t3. **Pressure, not spawn-spam.** Slow enemies, few attackers at a time, always one more behind you. Run, break line of sight, use doors, windows and ladders.\n21\t4. **Everything is a small decision.** Tight case, scarce ammo, a shop you want to visit, treasure you want to combine.\n22\t5. **Fast variety.** A new idea every few minutes: siege, conveyor hall, winch defence, crane boss, tide race, solo stealth, mech boss, boat escape.\n23\t6. **The jokes land because the danger is real.** Deadpan hero, ridiculous villain, horror underneath.\n24\t\n25\t### What we take from the source, and what we make new\n26\t\n27\tWe take its genre and mechanics. We don't recreate the game. Everything the player sees and hears is original.\n28\t\n29\t| What makes the source distinctive | Our original version |\n30\t| --- | --- |\n31\t| Over-the-shoulder laser aiming, standing still | The same mechanic (§4.2) |\n32\t| Locational hits leading to contextual melee | Same mechanic; our kick and takedown (§4.3) |\n33\t| Lone agent, rural cult, parasite | K&R adjuster, island fishing cult, neck-limpets called the Tithe |\n34\t| Rescued VIP who needs protecting | Dame Ottoline: rude, 74, deducts from your fee |\n35\t| Cloaked travelling merchant | Mr. Fathom, in a brass diving helmet with a green lantern |\n36\t| Grid inventory case | The Case: an Ostrander Mutual sample case (§4.5) |\n37\t| Radio support, villain taunts | Mr. Pym on the Claims Desk (with on-hold music); the Commodore, an unseen voice on island loudspeakers |\n38\t| Shootable collectibles | 15 Tannoys. Shooting one cuts the villain off mid-sentence |\n39\t| Save points | Telegraph keys in dry rooms |\n40\t| Big set-piece siege that ends abruptly | The Foghorn: the crowd drops its tools and walks into the sea |\n41\t| Instant-kill power-tool enemy | The Flenser, with a steam saw |\n42\t| Giant and castle-lord bosses | The Bosun and the crane; the Commodore's walking Bathysphere |\n43\t| Interactive cutscenes | QTEs with our own choreography |\n44\t| Campy one-liners | Insurance jokes, delivered deadpan |\n45\t| Chapter-end ranking | A Claim Form with a rubber stamp |\n46\t\n47\t**Originality guardrails. None of these may appear:** Leon, Ashley, Ada, Luis, Krauser, Salazar, Saddler, Hunnigan; Las Plagas, Los Ganados, Umbrella, Raccoon City; Spain or Spanish-speaking villagers; the source's lines (no catchphrases, no paraphrases of famous ones); its village, castle, island, lake or mine levels; its bosses (a giant with a parasite you climb onto, a lake monster fought with harpoons, a knife-fighting ex-comrade); its music, sounds, logo, UI art, typewriters or attaché-case look; a \"buying and selling, stranger\" merchant voice. If a beat you write feels like a copy of a specific scene, change it. \"Resident Evil 4\" appears only where TASK.md asks: the Source Information and the title riff.\n48\t\n49\t---\n50\t\n51\t## 2. World, cast, writing\n52\t\n53\t**Skerry Holm**, a treeless rock in the North Atlantic, fictional. Fishing, salt, a canning works. Generations ago the Vane shipping line made a pact with something under the island, the **Tithe**: safe passage for Vane ships in exchange for crew. Islanders drink brine from the Font in the old abbey; a **limpet** grows at the base of the skull and they become the **Congregation**. They still do their jobs, and their eyes glow. The Vane heir must sign the Tithe renewal in person, so Dame Ottoline was taken from her yacht.\n54\t\n55\t| Character | Who | Look |\n56\t| --- | --- | --- |\n57\t| **Max Ransom** | Maxine Ransom, field adjuster, Ostrander Mutual Assurance. Dry, tired, unkillable. Sunglasses at night (\"They're prescription\"). | Grey trench coat (long skirt), short sand-coloured hair, red scarf that flutters |\n58\t| **Dame Ottoline Vane** | 74, tiny, imperious. Keeps a ledger of deductions from your fee. Not helpless: she operates machinery and fights with a cane in her solo segment. | Fur coat, ebony cane, handbag, hat with a feather |\n59\t| **Commodore Anselm Thole** | Self-styled lord of the island, and its radio announcer. For the whole game he is only a huge, rich voice on the Tannoys, and he hates being interrupted. Never seen until the final boss: a gaunt old man in slippers, at a microphone, in an armchair inside his machine. | Scale 1.2, thin, peaked cap, far too many medals, dressing gown under the coat |\n60\t| **Mr. Fathom** | The merchant. Brass diving helmet with a round porthole, oilskin coat, green starboard lantern. Bubbling, oddly delighted. Never explains himself. | Unmistakable silhouette |\n61\t| **Mr. Pym** | Claims Desk, on the radio. Bureaucrat. Everything is a form. | Pixel portrait only |\n62\t| **The Bosun** | The Commodore's enforcer, 4 m tall, crusted with barnacles, drags an anchor. One bellow. | Scale 2.6 |\n63\t| **The Flenser** | Cannery man in rubber apron and sack hood with a steam-driven flensing saw. Mute. You hear him first. | Hood, saw, steam |\n64\t| **Mate Ansgar** | First mate, cannery bruiser with a gaff hook. One fight on the conveyor belt. | Pea coat, broad, hook |\n65\t| **Nils** | Boatman. Two lines, then the boat burns. | Sou'wester |\n66\t\n67\t### Writing style\n68\t\n69\tThe source's register: a B-movie played completely straight, with action-hero one-liners and a pompous villain. Ours is insurance-flavoured. Rules:\n70\t- Max never raises her voice. Her jokes are one short sentence, usually a policy term.\n71\t- The Commodore speaks in full theatrical sentences, in capitals, always over a loudspeaker, like a man reading the shipping forecast to the condemned.\n72\t- Dame Ottoline is never impressed and never thanks anyone.\n73\t- Mr. Pym is never alarmed.\n74\t- Horror is left unjoked: the Foghorn walk, the Cold Room, the crypt. No jokes during those beats until after them.\n75\t- All text must fit the HUD font (§11): no em dashes, no straight double quotes, no accents. Use `-` and `'`.\n76\t\n77\t### Script: use these lines, add barks in the same voice\n78\t\n79\t```\n80\tARRIVAL (pier, dusk)\n81\tNILS: This is as far as I go. Tide's wrong. Everything's wrong.\n82\tMAX: Ostrander Mutual thanks you for your cooperation.\n83\tNILS: Insurance? You came out here for insurance?\n84\tMAX: Someone has to read the small print.\n85\tPYM: Ms. Ransom, Claims Desk. Policy 7741, Dame Ottoline Vane. Kidnap and ransom, premium tier. Nine days, no demand. Locate the insured. Assess. Report.\n86\tMAX: And if the locals object?\n87\tPYM: Form 11-B. Incidental Violence. In triplicate.\n88\tCOMMODORE: ATTENTION, MOURNE QUAY. THIS IS YOUR COMMODORE. A SMALL WOMAN IN A LARGE COAT HAS COME ASHORE. KINDLY REMOVE HER.\n89\tMAX: Everyone's a critic.\n90\t\n91\tNET-MENDER (first cottage)\n92\tMAX: Evening. I'm looking for an old woman. Rich. Rude. Answers to Dame.\n93\tMENDER: (gibberish, subtitle) The tide takes what it's owed.\n94\tMAX: Is that a no?\n95\t(He stands. The band across his eyes lights.)\n96\tMAX: I'll put you down as a no.\n97\t(After the fight: a thump, orange light through the window. Nils's boat is burning.)\n98\tMAX: That'll be the excess.\n99\t\n100\tMR. FATHOM (first meeting)\n101\tFATHOM: Blub! Ahoy! A paying customer! Dry goods, wet goods, goods of no fixed description!\n102\tMAX: You're wearing a diving helmet.\n103\tFATHOM: And you're wearing a target, friend. Browse!\n104\tShop barks. Greet: Back already? Still breathing? Bad for the island, good for business. / Mind the drip! / Step aboard, step aboard.\n105\tBuy: Sold, and no refunds from the deep. / A fine choice for a short life.\n106\tSell: Half price and my condolences. / I'll find someone it fits. Someone with arms.\n107\tTune: She'll kick like a bosun now. / Tuned and tarred.\n108\tCan't afford: Credit? Friend, nobody here lives that long.\n109\tLeave: Come back alive. I've stock to move. / Mind the drip!\n110\t\n111\tSIEGE START (camera finds the crowd around the bonfire)\n112\tCOMMODORE: ALL HANDS TO THE SQUARE. BRING SOMETHING SHARP. BRING A FRIEND. BRING YOUR FRIEND'S SOMETHING SHARP.\n113\tMAX: Noted.\n114\t\n115\tTHE FOGHORN (siege ends. No music. No jokes until the last line)\n116\t(The horn sounds across the water, long and low. Everyone stops. Tools fall. They turn, and walk, and wade in.)\n117\tCOMMODORE: AH. THE TIDE WILL HAVE ITS SUPPER FIRST. DO STAY DRY, MISS RANSOM.\n118\tPYM: Ms. Ransom, are we looking at a mass claim? There's a separate form.\n119\tMAX: Fifty claimants, and not one forwarding address.\n120\t\n121\tCOLD ROOM (meeting the Dame. She sits on a crate of frozen herring, smoking)\n122\tDAME: You are nine days late and you are dripping.\n123\tMAX: Max Ransom. Ostrander Mutual.\n124\tDAME: Ransom. Is that a joke?\n125\tMAX: It's a surname. I've heard them all.\n126\tDAME: I pay your employers eleven thousand a year. I expected a regiment.\n127\tMAX: I'm the regiment. Can you walk?\n128\tDAME: I have walked out on better than you, young woman. Lead on. Mind the coat.\n129\t\n130\tAFTER THE BOSUN (rain; she lights a cigarette under the crane)\n131\tDAME: My great-grandfather signed the first Tithe. Every Vane ship that came home safe, the island kept a crew from it. That is your premium, Miss Ransom. That is what you insure.\n132\tMAX: That would've been useful on the application.\n133\tDAME: Nobody reads the application.\n134\t\n135\tCAUSEWAY\n136\tCOMMODORE: TIDE TABLES ARE PRINTED IN THE FOYER, MISS RANSOM. YOU HAVE FOUR MINUTES.\n137\tMAX: He printed tide tables.\n138\tDAME: He prints everything. He is a dreadful little man.\n139\t\n140\tCRYPT (the floor drops; control passes to the Dame)\n141\tDAME: Very well. I shall do it myself. As usual.\n142\tPYM: Dame Ottoline, this is the Claims Desk. Please hold.\n143\tDAME: I will not.\n144\t\n145\tFINAL BOSS\n146\tCOMMODORE: BEHOLD! THE BATHYSPHERE OF SAINT ORRIN! FORTY TONS OF DIVINE PLUMBING!\n147\tMAX: Are you covered for that?\n148\t(Hatch torn open. An old man in slippers, in an armchair, at a microphone.)\n149\tCOMMODORE: THIS IS AN OUTRAGE. I AM ON THE AIR.\n150\tMAX: Not for long."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95eb819f-952e-4afc-b710-af34f202e2ce","uuid":"9898eef4-f416-414b-8cd0-edb03b42e81b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T23:21:00.876Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-162051__xa8053f/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: an original over-the-shoulder action-horror game inspired by Resident Evil 4\n\nYou are building this without the director. This plan is the whole design: where it is specific, follow it; where it is silent, use your judgment and keep the tone. TASK.md still rules on the deliverable, its names, the title, the exit interview and how you may use this computer. The title is yours to pick the way TASK.md says, so nothing here names the game. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n\nFacts marked **[verified]** were run on this computer against engine kit 0.46.0 (the latest release at the time of planning). Don't re-derive them. If the kit you download is newer, read its `CHANGELOG.md` first.\n\nContents: 1 The game · 2 World, cast, writing · 3 Controls · 4 Systems · 5 Chapters and maps · 6 Bosses · 7 Night Shift and multiplayer · 8 Art · 9 Animation · 10 Audio · 11 UI and UX · 12 Dev mode · 13 Attract mode · 14 Architecture and engine facts · 15 Testing · 16 Build order · 17 Done, and what TASK.md asks for\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** 1979. Max Ransom, kidnap-and-ransom adjuster for a London insurer, goes alone to a storm-beaten North Atlantic island to recover a policyholder: Dame Ottoline Vane, 74, shipping magnate, the rudest woman alive. The islanders have barnacle-things growing out of the backs of their necks, the man who runs the place insults her over the loudspeakers, and a merchant in a brass diving helmet keeps turning up with guns. Played straight and silly at once: a B-movie with real dread and real precision shooting.\n\n**Genre:** third-person action-horror shooter with crowd control, resource management and an escort. **Perspective:** over-the-shoulder third person with a laser sight; letterboxed cinematic cameras for cutscenes and quick-time events; one on-rails shooting finale.\n\n**Pillars (when in doubt, these decide):**\n1. **Plant your feet.** You can't move while aiming. Every shot is a decision made while a crowd closes in.\n2. **Where you shoot matters.** Head and legs stagger; a stagger opens a melee follow-up that hits the whole crowd. The rhythm is shoot, kick, reposition.\n3. **Pressure, not spawn-spam.** Slow enemies, few attackers at a time, always one more behind you. Run, break line of sight, use doors, windows and ladders.\n4. **Everything is a small decision.** Tight case, scarce ammo, a shop you want to visit, treasure you want to combine.\n5. **Fast variety.** A new idea every few minutes: siege, conveyor hall, winch defence, crane boss, tide race, solo stealth, mech boss, boat escape.\n6. **The jokes land because the danger is real.** Deadpan hero, ridiculous villain, horror underneath.\n\n### What we take from the source, and what we make new\n\nWe take its genre and mechanics. We don't recreate the game. Everything the player sees and hears is original.\n\n| What makes the source distinctive | Our original version |\n| --- | --- |\n| Over-the-shoulder laser aiming, standing still | The same mechanic (§4.2) |\n| Locational hits leading to contextual melee | Same mechanic; our kick and takedown (§4.3) |\n| Lone agent, rural cult, parasite | K&R adjuster, island fishing cult, neck-limpets called the Tithe |\n| Rescued VIP who needs protecting | Dame Ottoline: rude, 74, deducts from your fee |\n| Cloaked travelling merchant | Mr. Fathom, in a brass diving helmet with a green lantern |\n| Grid inventory case | The Case: an Ostrander Mutual sample case (§4.5) |\n| Radio support, villain taunts | Mr. Pym on the Claims Desk (with on-hold music); the Commodore, an unseen voice on island loudspeakers |\n| Shootable collectibles | 15 Tannoys. Shooting one cuts the villain off mid-sentence |\n| Save points | Telegraph keys in dry rooms |\n| Big set-piece siege that ends abruptly | The Foghorn: the crowd drops its tools and walks into the sea |\n| Instant-kill power-tool enemy | The Flenser, with a steam saw |\n| Giant and castle-lord bosses | The Bosun and the crane; the Commodore's walking Bathysphere |\n| Interactive cutscenes | QTEs with our own choreography |\n| Campy one-liners | Insurance jokes, delivered deadpan |\n| Chapter-end ranking | A Claim Form with a rubber stamp |\n\n**Originality guardrails. None of these may appear:** Leon, Ashley, Ada, Luis, Krauser, Salazar, Saddler, Hunnigan; Las Plagas, Los Ganados, Umbrella, Raccoon City; Spain or Spanish-speaking villagers; the source's lines (no catchphrases, no paraphrases of famous ones); its village, castle, island, lake or mine levels; its bosses (a giant with a parasite you climb onto, a lake monster fought with harpoons, a knife-fighting ex-comrade); its music, sounds, logo, UI art, typewriters or attaché-case look; a \"buying and selling, stranger\" merchant voice. If a beat you write feels like a copy of a specific scene, change it. \"Resident Evil 4\" appears only where TASK.md asks: the Source Information and the title riff.\n\n---\n\n## 2. World, cast, writing\n\n**Skerry Holm**, a treeless rock in the North Atlantic, fictional. Fishing, salt, a canning works. Generations ago the Vane shipping line made a pact with something under the island, the **Tithe**: safe passage for Vane ships in exchange for crew. Islanders drink brine from the Font in the old abbey; a **limpet** grows at the base of the skull and they become the **Congregation**. They still do their jobs, and their eyes glow. The Vane heir must sign the Tithe renewal in person, so Dame Ottoline was taken from her yacht.\n\n| Character | Who | Look |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Max Ransom** | Maxine Ransom, field adjuster, Ostrander Mutual Assurance. Dry, tired, unkillable. Sunglasses at night (\"They're prescription\"). | Grey trench coat (long skirt), short sand-coloured hair, red scarf that flutters |\n| **Dame Ottoline Vane** | 74, tiny, imperious. Keeps a ledger of deductions from your fee. Not helpless: she operates machinery and fights with a cane in her solo segment. | Fur coat, ebony cane, handbag, hat with a feather |\n| **Commodore Anselm Thole** | Self-styled lord of the island, and its radio announcer. For the whole game he is only a huge, rich voice on the Tannoys, and he hates being interrupted. Never seen until the final boss: a gaunt old man in slippers, at a microphone, in an armchair inside his machine. | Scale 1.2, thin, peaked cap, far too many medals, dressing gown under the coat |\n| **Mr. Fathom** | The merchant. Brass diving helmet with a round porthole, oilskin coat, green starboard lantern. Bubbling, oddly delighted. Never explains himself. | Unmistakable silhouette |\n| **Mr. Pym** | Claims Desk, on the radio. Bureaucrat. Everything is a form. | Pixel portrait only |\n| **The Bosun** | The Commodore's enforcer, 4 m tall, crusted with barnacles, drags an anchor. One bellow. | Scale 2.6 |\n| **The Flenser** | Cannery man in rubber apron and sack hood with a steam-driven flensing saw. Mute. You hear him first. | Hood, saw, steam |\n| **Mate Ansgar** | First mate, cannery bruiser with a gaff hook. One fight on the conveyor belt. | Pea coat, broad, hook |\n| **Nils** | Boatman. Two lines, then the boat burns. | Sou'wester |\n\n### Writing style\n\nThe source's register: a B-movie played completely straight, with action-hero one-liners and a pompous villain. Ours is insurance-flavoured. Rules:\n- Max never raises her voice. Her jokes are one short sentence, usually a policy term.\n- The Commodore speaks in full theatrical sentences, in capitals, always over a loudspeaker, like a man reading the shipping forecast to the condemned.\n- Dame Ottoline is never impressed and never thanks anyone.\n- Mr. Pym is never alarmed.\n- Horror is left unjoked: the Foghorn walk, the Cold Room, the crypt. No jokes during those beats until after them.\n- All text must fit the HUD font (§11): no em dashes, no straight double quotes, no accents. Use `-` and `'`.\n\n### Script: use these lines, add barks in the same voice\n\n```\nARRIVAL (pier, dusk)\nNILS: This is as far as I go. Tide's wrong. Everything's wrong.\nMAX: Ostrander Mutual thanks you for your cooperation.\nNILS: Insurance? You came out here for insurance?\nMAX: Someone has to read the small print.\nPYM: Ms. Ransom, Claims Desk. Policy 7741, Dame Ottoline Vane. Kidnap and ransom, premium tier. Nine days, no demand. Locate the insured. Assess. Report.\nMAX: And if the locals object?\nPYM: Form 11-B. Incidental Violence. In triplicate.\nCOMMODORE: ATTENTION, MOURNE QUAY. THIS IS YOUR COMMODORE. A SMALL WOMAN IN A LARGE COAT HAS COME ASHORE. KINDLY REMOVE HER.\nMAX: Everyone's a critic.\n\nNET-MENDER (first cottage)\nMAX: Evening. I'm looking for an old woman. Rich. Rude. Answers to Dame.\nMENDER: (gibberish, subtitle) The tide takes what it's owed.\nMAX: Is that a no?\n(He stands. The band across his eyes lights.)\nMAX: I'll put you down as a no.\n(After the fight: a thump, orange light through the window. Nils's boat is burning.)\nMAX: That'll be the excess.\n\nMR. FATHOM (first meeting)\nFATHOM: Blub! Ahoy! A paying customer! Dry goods, wet goods, goods of no fixed description!\nMAX: You're wearing a diving helmet.\nFATHOM: And you're wearing a target, friend. Browse!\nShop barks. Greet: Back already? Still breathing? Bad for the island, good for business. / Mind the drip! / Step aboard, step aboard.\nBuy: Sold, and no refunds from the deep. / A fine choice for a short life.\nSell: Half price and my condolences. / I'll find someone it fits. Someone with arms.\nTune: She'll kick like a bosun now. / Tuned and tarred.\nCan't afford: Credit? Friend, nobody here lives that long.\nLeave: Come back alive. I've stock to move. / Mind the drip!\n\nSIEGE START (camera finds the crowd around the bonfire)\nCOMMODORE: ALL HANDS TO THE SQUARE. BRING SOMETHING SHARP. BRING A FRIEND. BRING YOUR FRIEND'S SOMETHING SHARP.\nMAX: Noted.\n\nTHE FOGHORN (siege ends. No music. No jokes until the last line)\n(The horn sounds across the water, long and low. Everyone stops. Tools fall. They turn, and walk, and wade in.)\nCOMMODORE: AH. THE TIDE WILL HAVE ITS SUPPER FIRST. DO STAY DRY, MISS RANSOM.\nPYM: Ms. Ransom, are we looking at a mass claim? There's a separate form.\nMAX: Fifty claimants, and not one forwarding address.\n\nCOLD ROOM (meeting the Dame. She sits on a crate of frozen herring, smoking)\nDAME: You are nine days late and you are dripping.\nMAX: Max Ransom. Ostrander Mutual.\nDAME: Ransom. Is that a joke?\nMAX: It's a surname. I've heard them all.\nDAME: I pay your employers eleven thousand a year. I expected a regiment.\nMAX: I'm the regiment. Can you walk?\nDAME: I have walked out on better than you, young woman. Lead on. Mind the coat.\n\nAFTER THE BOSUN (rain; she lights a cigarette under the crane)\nDAME: My great-grandfather signed the first Tithe. Every Vane ship that came home safe, the island kept a crew from it. That is your premium, Miss Ransom. That is what you insure.\nMAX: That would've been useful on the application.\nDAME: Nobody reads the application.\n\nCAUSEWAY\nCOMMODORE: TIDE TABLES ARE PRINTED IN THE FOYER, MISS RANSOM. YOU HAVE FOUR MINUTES.\nMAX: He printed tide tables.\nDAME: He prints everything. He is a dreadful little man.\n\nCRYPT (the floor drops; control passes to the Dame)\nDAME: Very well. I shall do it myself. As usual.\nPYM: Dame Ottoline, this is the Claims Desk. Please hold.\nDAME: I will not.\n\nFINAL BOSS\nCOMMODORE: BEHOLD! THE BATHYSPHERE OF SAINT ORRIN! FORTY TONS OF DIVINE PLUMBING!\nMAX: Are you covered for that?\n(Hatch torn open. An old man in slippers, in an armchair, at a microphone.)\nCOMMODORE: THIS IS AN OUTRAGE. I AM ON THE AIR.\nMAX: Not for long.","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":885}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01He5RNQWf2S5nDNY9xFxDhr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
